1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.883
You need a computer powerful enough for your research.

2
00:00:03.883 --> 00:00:07.767
You are told it is not available.

3
00:00:07.767 --> 00:00:11.650
What do you do next?

4
00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:15.533
Not a laptop.

5
00:00:15.533 --> 00:00:19.417
Not a machine for typing reports.

6
00:00:19.417 --> 00:00:23.300
A machine that could help model weather, materials, and enormous scientific problems.

7
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:30.333
In the nineteen eighties, access to advanced imported supercomputers became a serious obstacle for India.

8
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:37.367
The easy response would have been to wait for permission.

9
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:44.400
But a country that needs answers cannot always wait for somebody else's machine.

10
00:00:44.400 --> 00:01:06.600
The Centre for Development of Advanced Computing, or C-DAC, was established in nineteen eighty-eight against the background of denied supercomputer imports.

11
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:28.800
Its answer would become the PARAM family.

12
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:41.025
That is parallel computing.

13
00:01:41.025 --> 00:01:53.250
Instead of expecting one processor to perform every calculation, a supercomputer distributes work across many processors.

14
00:01:53.250 --> 00:02:05.475
Each handles a portion.

15
00:02:05.475 --> 00:02:17.700
Then the results are brought together.

16
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.360
And there is a third problem.

17
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:29.020
Think of a relay race.

18
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:34.680
A fast runner cannot finish the race if the baton is dropped.

19
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:40.340
In parallel computing, software decides how work is divided, how tasks communicate, and when partial answers are combined.

20
00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:46.000
Poor coordination can erase the advantage of all that hardware.

21
00:02:46.000 --> 00:03:06.550
This is why a supercomputer is not simply a room filled with computers.

22
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:27.100
It is an agreement between hardware, networks, memory, software, cooling and people who understand the problems being solved.

23
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:31.817
Here is a fictional scenario.

24
00:03:31.817 --> 00:03:36.533
A weather model is divided among many processors.

25
00:03:36.533 --> 00:03:41.250
One processor finishes early, but another is still calculating.

26
00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:45.967
The whole system must manage that imbalance.

27
00:03:45.967 --> 00:03:50.683
If communication and scheduling are poor, adding more processors may barely improve the result.

28
00:03:50.683 --> 00:03:55.400
That is the hidden art behind the machine.

29
00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:03.450
Today's frontier is different.

30
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:11.500
Supercomputing now depends on accelerators, high-speed networks, energy efficiency and software tuned to new architectures.

31
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:19.550
Global supply chains still matter.

32
00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:27.600
Independence is a moving target.

33
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:35.400
The barrier was not defeated by pretending it did not exist.

34
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:43.200
It was answered by learning how the entire system worked.
